1
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:04,980
Trunked radio is how hundreds of user
groups share a handful of frequencies — by

2
00:00:04,980 --> 00:00:07,609
never owning any of them.

3
00:00:07,959 --> 00:00:09,480
Here is the problem it solves.

4
00:00:09,600 --> 00:00:13,362
Under conventional radio, every group gets
its own fixed frequency.

5
00:00:13,482 --> 00:00:15,618
Police dispatch is always on channel A.

6
00:00:15,738 --> 00:00:17,596
Fire is always on channel B.

7
00:00:17,716 --> 00:00:19,864
Public works is always on channel C.

8
00:00:19,984 --> 00:00:24,117
Simple — but wasteful, because most of the
time, most of those channels are silent.

9
00:00:24,467 --> 00:00:25,953
Radio traffic is bursty.

10
00:00:26,073 --> 00:00:29,062
Any one group transmits only a small
fraction of the time, and groups rarely

11
00:00:29,062 --> 00:00:32,052
all key up at once.

12
00:00:32,172 --> 00:00:35,579
A city with thirty groups on thirty
frequencies is paying for thirty channels

13
00:00:35,579 --> 00:00:38,987
and using maybe four of them in any given
second.

14
00:00:39,337 --> 00:00:40,661
Trunking flips the model.

15
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:43,090
Take a small pool of channels — say five —
and let a computer hand them out on

16
00:00:43,090 --> 00:00:45,401
demand.

17
00:00:45,521 --> 00:00:48,493
When someone keys up, their radio asks for
a channel.

18
00:00:48,613 --> 00:00:52,340
A coordinator called the control channel
picks a free one, announces the

19
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:56,067
assignment, and every radio in that user's
group retunes to it together.

20
00:00:56,187 --> 00:00:58,805
When the talker is done and a hang timer
expires, the channel goes back into the

21
00:00:58,805 --> 00:01:01,423
pool for the next call.

22
00:01:01,773 --> 00:01:03,898
No group owns a frequency anymore.

23
00:01:04,018 --> 00:01:07,588
What a group owns is an identity — a
talkgroup — and the system finds a

24
00:01:07,588 --> 00:01:11,158
physical channel for each call, one call
at a time.

25
00:01:11,508 --> 00:01:15,676
The reason this works is pure statistics —
the same queuing theory that lets a

26
00:01:15,676 --> 00:01:19,844
telephone exchange serve a whole city with
far fewer lines than subscribers.

27
00:01:19,964 --> 00:01:23,099
Traffic engineers measure offered load in
erlangs and size the pool so that the

28
00:01:23,099 --> 00:01:26,233
probability of a call finding every
channel busy — the grade of service —

29
00:01:26,233 --> 00:01:29,368
stays low.

30
00:01:29,488 --> 00:01:33,656
If it ever happens anyway, the system
queues the request or plays a busy tone.

31
00:01:34,006 --> 00:01:37,156
The payoff is dramatic: a well-sized pool
of five channels can comfortably serve

32
00:01:37,156 --> 00:01:40,307
dozens of talkgroups that would each have
demanded a permanent frequency under the

33
00:01:40,307 --> 00:01:43,457
conventional model.

34
00:01:43,806 --> 00:01:45,907
The idea comes in several shapes.

35
00:01:46,028 --> 00:01:51,740
FDMA systems like P25 Phase 1 give each
call its own frequency.

36
00:01:51,860 --> 00:01:56,533
TDMA systems like DMR and TETRA split each
frequency into timeslots, so one channel

37
00:01:56,533 --> 00:02:01,206
carries two or even four calls at once.

38
00:02:01,326 --> 00:02:04,280
And a single site can stand alone, or be
linked with others into a wide-area

39
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:07,235
network covering a whole state.

40
00:02:07,355 --> 00:02:09,758
We will meet all of these later in the
course.

41
00:02:10,109 --> 00:02:12,640
For a scanner, trunking changes
everything.

42
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:16,231
You cannot just park on one frequency,
because the conversation you care about

43
00:02:16,231 --> 00:02:19,703
scatters across the pool, call by call.

44
00:02:19,822 --> 00:02:23,032
GopherTrunk deals with this the same way
the radios do: it decodes the

45
00:02:23,032 --> 00:02:26,242
coordinator's announcements and follows
each call to wherever it lands — which

46
00:02:26,242 --> 00:02:29,453
means one software-defined radio can
reconstruct every conversation on the

47
00:02:29,453 --> 00:02:32,663
system at once.

48
00:02:33,813 --> 00:02:38,422
So: trunked radio shares a small pool of
channels among many groups.

49
00:02:38,542 --> 00:02:42,594
A computer assigns a free channel to each
call and takes it back afterward.

50
00:02:42,714 --> 00:02:45,791
And what a group keeps is not a frequency
but an identity.

51
00:02:45,911 --> 00:02:47,803
The full write-up is linked below.
